    <DataNode elementId="AT4G24220-MONOMER" textLabel="enone reductase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="A8MRU3" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="215" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE, dreher">VEP1 is a member of the short-chain dehydrogenase / reductase family of enzymes. A purified recombinant version of the protein can catalyze the stereo-specific reduction of |FRAME: PROGESTERONE progesterone| |CITS: [19166903]|, though this is not expected to be a substrate for the enzyme &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; in |FRAME: ORG-5993 &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; col| plants. It can also act on several additional &amp;Delta;-4,5-steroids that have a 3-oxo group, including |FRAME: ANDROST4ENE androst-4-ene-3,17-dione|, 21-acetyl cortexone, |FRAME: CORTISONE cortisone| and canarigenone |CITS: [19166903]|. Furthermore, it is active against a number of smaller molecules including |FRAME: CPD-282 cyclohex-2-enone|, but-1-en-3-one, and ethyl propenoate. This suggests that an activated carbon-carbon double bond is an important determinant of VEP1 substrate preference and that VPE1 may play a broader role as an enone reductase |CITS: [20598327]|. To date, the biological substrates of VEP1 are not known, though there is some speculation that VEP1 may participate in the formation of 5&amp;beta;-configured phytoecdysteroids because these compounds have been found in Arabidopsis |CITS: [19166903]|. Interestingly, mutants with defects in VEP1 expression have abnormal leaf venation patterns |CITS: [11917087]|. Based on gel filtration assays and native PAGE, VEP1 had an apparent molecular weight of 110 kD, suggesting that it forms a homodimer. However, this has not been confirmed experimentally |CITS: [19166903]|.</Comment>
